# Rank 1185 / CVE-2025-68696: httparty ignores base_uri whenever the path
# argument is itself an absolute URL (GHSA-hm5p-x4rq-38w4). A class that
# pins base_uri to a trusted host and sets an API key header still routes to
# whatever host a caller-supplied absolute path names, so an attacker who
# controls that path value redirects the request -- and the configured
# header -- to a server of their choosing (CWE-918, CWE-20). Fix 0529bcd630
# is the genuine patch, read from its own diff, not a release bump: it adds
# Request#validate_uri_safety!, called from Request#uri right after `path`
# resolves to `new_uri` and before the query string is attached, comparing
# new_uri.host against the configured base_uri's host and raising
# UnsafeURIError on a mismatch (skippable with skip_uri_validation:, and
# skipped outright on redirects); the accompanying spec asserts exactly the
# advisory's PoC shape. No pool correction needed.
#
# The advisory's own PoC is httparty's documented usage idiom -- a class
# that `include`s HTTParty and calls its class methods on itself from the
# outside:
#
#   class RepositoryClient
#     include HTTParty
#     base_uri 'http://example.test/api/v1/repositories/'
#     headers 'X-API-KEY' => '1234567890'
#   end
#
#   post '/issue' do
#     request_body = JSON.parse(request.body.read)
#     RepositoryClient.get(request_body['repository_id']).body
#   end
#
# vyql match on this shape labels request.body/request.body.read as
# code.HttpInput and nothing else -- RepositoryClient.get is not labelled at
# all. vyql resolve confirms the call itself is unresolved
# (RepositoryClient.get.body, "callee not traced"), and vyql trace from
# code.HttpInput to code.UrlFetch dead-ends at that same call: "no outgoing
# flow (callee not traced -- external or cross-package)". vyql scan reports
# 0 findings on this file, and scanning the real jnunemaker/httparty
# checkout itself (both at 0529bcd630^ and at 0529bcd630) reports nothing
# SSRF-related either, because the exploitable call site is application
# code that merely depends on httparty, not the gem's own source.
#
# The direct call form the library's README also documents --
# HTTParty.get(path, base_uri: '...') with no wrapping class -- already
# fires VYQL-RF-001 (vypr.request_forgery.Ssrf: taint core.UserControlledData
# -> code.UrlFetch), because urlFetch119/urlFetch120 in
# vyql/bindings/ruby/m5a48bf47.vyql key on the literal call path
# "HTTParty.get"/"HTTParty.post". That binding cannot reach the subclass
# idiom: "HTTParty.get" is dotted-segment text matching, and a subclass's
# own name (RepositoryClient here) never contains the literal segment
# "HTTParty" no matter what it includes.
#
# ATTENTION, engine gap. Recognizing the subclass idiom needs the engine to
# know a given class body includes HTTParty, and Ruby's context-token
# extraction cannot express that today. `include HTTParty` parses as a
# `command` node; rbConv.dotted (internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/ruby.go)
# returns only the command's method name ("include") for that node kind and
# drops its argument entirely, so no call_path, call, or selector token for
# "HTTParty" is ever produced. rbStructuredContextTokens's "call" case
# builds every token from `dotted()`, so this loss is unconditional: no
# combination of node.context.callPath/call/selector/literal can see which
# module a class includes. Confirmed by reading the full walk in
# rbStructuredContextTokens -- only assignment/binary/pair/call/
# element_reference/string/regex nodes emit tokens, and calls made without
# parentheses (Ruby's `command` node kind, which is how `include Foo`,
# `extend Foo`, and `base_uri '...'` are all written) never contribute their
# argument as a fact. Other frontends already solve this: c.go, php.go,
# scala.go and rust.go all emit "call_arg:" tokens (matched by
# node.context.callArg) for a call's arguments, not just its callee chain;
# ruby.go has no equivalent. Adding one would let a class-scope binding
# require both a `call` token for "include" and a `callArg` token for
# "HTTParty" in the same class body, discriminating the mixin generically
# (any class documented as `include HTTParty` per the library's own README,
# not this CVE's class name) rather than matching on RepositoryClient by
# name. That is a Go change to the extractor, not a binding or a rule, so it
# is not attempted here; entered in ENGINE-GAPS.md with this file's minimal
# pair as the regression test to isolate it.
#
# No definition change shipped.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-precision-limit, the documented
# subclass idiom produces no finding at all today

test "rank1185: HTTParty.get called directly with a tainted absolute-URL path and a configured base_uri fires the generic SSRF rule"
  lang ruby
  file app.rb
  expect VYQL-RF-001
  code
  ```
  require 'sinatra'
  require 'httparty'

  post '/issue' do
    request_body = JSON.parse(request.body.read)
    HTTParty.get(request_body['repository_id'], base_uri: 'http://example.test/api/v1/repositories/').body
  end
  ```

test "rank1185: the same direct call with a hardcoded URL carries no taint and does not fire"
  lang ruby
  file app.rb
  reject VYQL-RF-001
  code
  ```
  require 'sinatra'
  require 'httparty'

  post '/issue' do
    HTTParty.get('http://example.test/api/v1/repositories/default', base_uri: 'http://example.test/api/v1/repositories/').body
  end
  ```

test "rank1185: the advisory's own subclass idiom (include HTTParty, base_uri pinned, .get called on the class) reaches no sink at all"
  lang ruby
  file app.rb
  reject VYQL-RF-001
  code
  ```
  require 'sinatra'
  require 'httparty'

  class RepositoryClient
    include HTTParty
    base_uri 'http://example.test/api/v1/repositories/'
    headers 'X-API-KEY' => '1234567890'
  end

  post '/issue' do
    request_body = JSON.parse(request.body.read)
    RepositoryClient.get(request_body['repository_id']).body
  end
  ```
